==============================================================================
  PART 2 -- F2: period and collision structure of the input channels
==============================================================================
  64-bit input word, 64-bit output. Chance agreement of two outputs: 2^-64.
  birthday sub-domain: 32 low bits, N = 2^18 inputs; a period inside it would give
  N^2/2^33 = 8 colliding pairs on average => detection probability 99.9665%.

  [CH-A keyed T4-Q30, input = PUBLIC SEED]
     [MEASURED] XOR 2^b, inputs below 2^52 : exact periods at bits {32..51}, partial {}  [matches pre-registered]
                (pre-registered: {32..51} exact, no partial)
     [MEASURED] XOR 2^b, inputs above 2^52 : exact periods at bits {}, partial {}  [matches pre-registered]
     [MEASURED] ADD 2^b (mod 2^64)         : below 2^52 exact {32..51} partial {}; above 2^52 exact {} partial {}
                (an exact additive period forces dependence on the low bits only, so it must
                 reappear among the XOR rows; this row is a cross-check of the row above)
     [MEASURED] 64 random 64-bit XOR offsets, 32 inputs each: best agreement 0/32 and 0/32
     [MEASURED] closure: 16 of 16 random XOR-combinations of those bits are exact periods
     => exact XOR-period subgroup of dimension 20 on inputs below 2^52.
     [MEASURED] same scan under a second key: exact periods at bits {32..51} -> IDENTICAL: the period does not depend on the key
     [INSPECTION] t_k = hash_seed(seed)*omega_k mod 2^32 and hash_seed is the identity up to 2^52
                  (sidecar, mcl_q30_seed_state4 and mcl_q30_seed_state2, Legacy rule; mcl_core.hpp,
                  hash_seed and mcl_q30_init_state): the seed enters the state modulo 2^32, so the
                  engine has 2^32 initial states. This is the DEFAULT rule. The opt-in Hashed rule
                  of sidecar v1.0.7 is not scanned here.
     reading: a PUBLIC, key-independent period. Simon's algorithm would recover a period the
