==============================================================================
  PART 3 -- F3: the legacy transaction tag as an FX construction
==============================================================================
  Tag = MCL_T2( hash_tx(tx) XOR nonce(ctr) XOR W(S_device), p, q )      (variant)
  A variant of the superseded input-composition form: the published mcl_txn_verify.cpp has no
  word W; its device secret is the pair (p, q).
  SCOPE: the LEGACY tag only. Paper 5 Eq. (3) carries the secret in the twelve weights and has
         no seed-XOR whitening; nothing in this part applies to it.

  S1 [INSPECTION] FX shape  Enc(u) = E_k(u XOR k1), k = (p,q), k1 = W: the whitening is additive and
                  depends on the key only.
  [CONTROL] PC-4 FX period: present for the right key only PASS  f = Tag XOR E_k' has period W on 32/32 inputs for k' = k, on 0/32 for k' != k
     (forced by the FX shape -- a demonstration on the real engine, not a discovery.)
  S2 [INSPECTION] Leander-May Theorem 2 assumes g(k,.) is a random FUNCTION, not a permutation.
                  MCL_T2 being a generator does not block the attack. "E is a keyed permutation"
                  is NOT a precondition of the theorem.
  S3 [INSPECTION] nonce(ctr) = fmix64(ctr) is a composition of bijections, so for any fixed tx
                  ctr -> hash_tx(tx) XOR nonce(ctr) is a bijection onto the 64-bit words.
     [MEASURED] target word reached through the counter on 32/32 random targets;
                fmix64 inverse: constants multiply to 1, round trip 100000/100000
     => every engine-input word is addressable, PROVIDED the attacker chooses the counter.
  S4 [NOT MEASURABLE HERE] the access model. Theorem 2 needs superposition queries to the tag
                  oracle (Q2). The counter is held by the device and only moves forward, so a
                  deployed token answers neither chosen counters nor superpositions. THIS is the
                  open assumption -- a property of the protocol, not of the map.
  complexities [DERIVED from the cited theorems; m = log2(coprime pairs in [2,1e9]^2) = 59.08, n = 64]
     Grover on (p,q,W), no structure used        : 2^61.5
     Q2, Leander-May Theorem 2                   : 2^29.5 x O(m+n) queries  -- W adds nothing
     Q1 (classical queries), offline Simon T^2.D = 2^(n+m):
         D = 2^16 -> T = 2^53.5 ;  D = 2^32 -> T = 2^45.5 ;  balanced T = D = 2^41.0
     Q1 needs the queried words to fill an affine subspace, i.e. chosen inputs.
  whitening word W = sdevice_fold(S_device)
     [INSPECTION] W is one 64-bit word: S_device contributes at most 64 bits whatever its length.
     [MEASURED] 2^22 random S_device -> 0 equal W: effective image size at least 2^41.4 words
                (95% confidence; 2^40.8 at 99%). A LOWER bound on the image, nothing more.

  VERDICT for the legacy tag: FX-shaped. In the chosen-input superposition model the whitening
  word adds no quantum security over Grover on (p,q). What stands between that statement and a
  deployed token is the access model (S4), not any property of the map. Paper 5's 46-56 bit
  figure for a seed-borne secret is an UPPER bound and must not be read as a floor.

==============================================================================
  F4 -- generic collision search  [GENERIC: holds for any function of that size]
==============================================================================
  n     what has that width                  classical 2^(n/2)  BHT 2^(n/3)    CNS 2^(2n/5)
  64    cascade state; legacy input word     2^32.0             2^21.3         2^25.6
  128   T4-Q30 / VDF128-T4 state             2^64.0             2^42.7         2^51.2
  256   tag; SHA-256 commitment              2^128.0            2^85.3         2^102.4
  BHT needs 2^(n/3) quantum-accessible memory; CNS needs O(n) qubits and 2^(n/5) classical memory.
  Nothing was measured here. "No collisions" cannot be claimed for any finite-state function.

